## Findings

I reproduced the failure locally (`GVM_TCK_LV=6.0.0 GVM_TCK_NATIVE_IMAGE_MODE=current-defaults ./gradlew clean test -Pcoordinates=org.springframework:spring-aspects:5.3.31`) since the issue's log was truncated right before the actual failure detail.

**Real failure** (2 of 8 tests): CGLIB tries to define proxy classes for Spring's own internal `@Configuration` infrastructure classes at native-image runtime:
```
org.springframework.cglib.core.CodeGenerationException: com.oracle.svm.core.jdk.UnsupportedFeatureError-->
Classes cannot be defined at runtime by default when using ahead-of-time Native Image compilation.
Tried to define class: org/springframework/transaction/aspectj/AspectJTransactionManagementConfiguration$$SpringCGLIB$$0
```
and the same for `org/springframework/context/annotation/aspectj/SpringConfiguredConfiguration$$SpringCGLIB$$0`.

This is Spring 6's `ConfigurationClassPostProcessor` CGLIB-enhancing its own internal config classes (imported via `@EnableTransactionManagement`/`@EnableSpringConfigured`), unrelated to the user's own `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)` test classes.

**This is not an unsupportable mechanism** — this repo already has a proven, working pattern for exactly this CGLIB-proxy-at-build-time problem (`PredefinedCglibProxyClassesGenerator` used in `org.springframework/spring-context/6.0.0`, `spring-aop/6.0.0`, `spring-shell-core-autoconfigure/4.0.2`, `spring-cloud-function-context/5.0.x`): a JavaExec task runs the code path in a plain JVM before `nativeTestCompile`, dumping the exact `...$$SpringCGLIB$$N.class` bytes into the test classes dir so they're ordinary compiled classes by the time native-image builds, plus reflection metadata (`condition.typeReached: org.springframework.cglib.proxy.Enhancer`) and the `spring.objenesis.ignore=true` system property (test + native runtime arg). A viable fallback exists, so per the grounding rules this is repairable, not `skip_unsupported`.

